  "detail": "HTML references are decoded only once, outside the layer loop (publicationMatches); base64 is spliced into the loop; and auditDiff adds a third pass that removes each entry's literal spelling and re-checks. Each review round has found another composition that slips through (most recently percent or HTML encoding around base64, and literal redaction destroying evidence before decoding). Replace the special cases with one decoder and one decision point: (1) in normalizedLayers, every round applies all supported decoders: percent runs, \\u escapes, HTML character references (with the whitespace collapse publicationMatches does now), then base64 runs whose decoded text contains a protected value. Repeat until nothing changes, within the existing round limit (not settling stays a refusal, as now). Replacing only matching base64 runs keeps unrelated base64-looking words from affecting convergence. publicationMatches/checkPublicationText then use that matcher directly. (2) In auditDiff, decide on the terminal-escape-stripped raw file and detail, before any literal redaction, with that matcher over private entries plus registered credentials: a matching file becomes `[redacted filename]`, a matching detail `[redacted diagnostic]`, otherwise keep the text. Remove the per-entry redactLiteral pass and the separate raw-base64 pass. (3) Encodings nested inside a decoded base64 run (base64 of percent- or HTML-encoded text) stay out of scope: say so in a short comment where base64 is decoded. (4) Add one table-driven test covering, for a private entry and a registered credential, in an added file's name and in a finding's detail: percent(HTML(x)), HTML(percent(x)), percent(base64(x)), HTML-escaped base64, base64 at a non-zero byte offset (`user:` + x), and an unrelated base64-looking word that must neither redact nor refuse. Keep the source change small: the issue budgeted about 30 lines, and this version is about 70."
 }
